DCL — Data Control Language

Definition:
DCL (Data Control Language) is a part of SQL used to control access and permissions on database objects.

DCL is mainly used by database administrators to decide:

Who can access what, and what operations they are allowed to perform?

The main DCL commands are:
GRANT
REVOKE


1. GRANT
What is GRANT?

GRANT is used to give specific privileges to a user or role.

Syntax:
GRANT privilege
ON database_object
TO user;

Example
GRANT SELECT
ON company.Employees
TO analyst_user;

This gives analyst_user permission to retrieve data from the Employees table.

Grant Multiple Privileges
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE
ON company.Employees
TO analyst_user;

Now the user can:
Read data
Insert data
Update data

depending on the database's permission model.

Grant All Privileges

Some database systems support:

GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES
ON company.*
TO admin_user;

This gives broad privileges on objects within the specified database, subject to the database system's rules.

Use broad privileges carefully.

2. REVOKE:

What is REVOKE?
REVOKE is used to remove previously granted privileges.

Syntax:
REVOKE privilege
ON database_object
FROM user;
Example
REVOKE UPDATE
ON company.Employees
FROM analyst_user;

Now the user no longer has the UPDATE privilege on that object.

Real-World Example
Imagine a company has three users:

Admin
   ↓
Full database access

Data Analyst
   ↓
SELECT access

Intern
   ↓
Limited access

The analyst may only need to read data.

Query:

GRANT SELECT
ON company.Sales
TO analyst_user;

There is no need to give the analyst permission to delete the company's sales data.

This is an example of least privilege.
